// logic.memql -- the __PRODUCT__ product pack (starter). Pure DSL, no Go.
//
// The callable body the __PRODUCT_CAMEL__GreetOnGreetingCreate automation runs on a graph event.
// An automation runs under a SYSTEM actor, so the OWNED __PRODUCT_CAMEL__MyGreetings query (gated
// on ownerUserId==actor.userId) would match ZERO rows here -- instead this body
// reads the NEW greeting's owner's greetings by passing the owner id straight
// from the event payload (event.payload.ownerUserId, the field the __PRODUCT_CAMEL__CreateGreeting
// mutation stamped) into the owner-parameterized __PRODUCT_CAMEL__GreetingsForOwner query. This
// exercises the event -> automation -> logic -> query path with ZERO product Go
// and no integration executor, and returns REAL rows (the just-created greeting
// is in the result).
//
// A logic body invokes a query/mutation by its bare name with NAMED args
// (`__PRODUCT_CAMEL__GreetingsForOwner(ownerUserId: ...)`, empty = `name()`) and reads the event
// as `args.event.payload.*` -- the canonical grammar (see the engine's
// authoring-rules.md + examples/deploypack; object-literal `name({...})` args
// are retired). A real product often writes a row or calls an
// integration.<name>.* capability here; the latter needs a thin bff pack
// (bespoke Go), tracked in memql-project#11. Prefer pure DSL first.

use __PRODUCT_ID__.queries.{ __PRODUCT_CAMEL__GreetingsForOwner }

@description("Pure-DSL reaction to a greeting-created event: reads the greetings owned by the new greeting's owner (owner id taken from event.payload.ownerUserId, since an automation's actor is the system, not the owner). Demonstrates a zero-Go automation body that returns real rows.")
logic __PRODUCT_CAMEL__OnGreetingCreated {
  args {
    event  object  @required
  }
  return query __PRODUCT_CAMEL__GreetingsForOwner(ownerUserId: args.event.payload.ownerUserId)
}
